+# Final review: cluster 13 (src-protocol), epic ht-ma6i
+
+## Review basis
+
+Source: base cf13ed76c1c2871b321ad290d03229cb785c400e, head 0755e609de0328e6d73584dd65eb2854e1fd7bf7, both verified as commits. The caller checkout stood at that head with a clean status at the start and at the last read; no change was observed.
+
+Inputs: slices 000042 to 000050 (`src/protocol/authority.rs`, `capabilities.rs`, `commands.rs`, `ids.rs`, `output.rs`, `output_compact.rs`, `pagination.rs`, `results.rs`, `service.rs`), the digest, and the design at `docs/superpowers/runs/2026-11-04-graph-clone-identity/2026-11-04-graph-clone-identity-design.md`. Other files were opened only to check a named risk.
+
+Evidence was read at the head SHA. `scripts/check-wire-compat --base <base>` passed (0 violations, 0 stale) and `scripts/check-harness-neutral` passed (0 violations), both run in the caller worktree. No cargo build or test ran in this review: the epic's owner runs the full suite once, so the tests below are named from source and were not executed here.
+
+## Conformance to spec 4.5 and 4.2
+
+- Every name in spec 4.5 matches: the five operations, their request and result shapes, `ContinuityConflictKind` with its snake_case wire names, `ContinuityKeyBinding`, `SeatContinuityKey`, `ContinuityKeyConflict`, `SeatRetirementState`, `ContinuityAlert`. `SeatInspection.continuity_key` is boxed; the wire shape is the struct, as a test pins.
+- `ContinuityKey` comes from the shared `id!` macro, whose `parse` is exactly the 1 to 128 ASCII-graphic rule, also applied on deserialization. The `continuity_key` field on `ResolveSeat`, `ContinuityCheckIn` and `CheckIn` carries the specified serde attributes, so a keyless request's bytes do not change, and a unit test pins that for all three.
+- `ServiceSessionLevel` is the one exhaustive match. `service_connection.rs` keeps the negotiated level and answers `Unsupported` naming the required capability when `level < required`. `tests/daemon/service_session_capability.rs` covers v1 and v2 refusing the v3 operations and v3 serving every generation.
+- `CursorScope::ContinuityAlerts` is appended at the end of `SCOPE_CODES`, keeping existing wire codes stable. `tests/store/continuity_keys.rs` refuses a foreign cursor.
+- `ErrorCode::ContinuityKeyPending` is in the variant list, has no default class (pinned in `tests/protocol/error_class.rs`), exits 1 through the existing catch-all in `api_exit_code`, and is definitive under `is_continuity_refusal`, so a claimless refusal is neither retried nor kept. That is what spec 4.2's gap fill asks for.
+- `COOPERATIVE_CONTINUITY_KEY_PROVENANCE` is written by `store/seats/continuity.rs:940`, allowed by the schema delta's `kind` CHECK, and defined in TRUST-POLICY.md, per AGENTS.md's trust rule.
+- Text output: `seat inspect` drops the raw `continuity_key` and `retirement_state` fields and prints the two compact lines. The stated sizes are right (6 bytes plus key and owner; 28 bytes). The key and owner are `id!` values, so neither can inject a line. A final retirement prints nothing new.
+
+## Risks checked and cleared
+
+- `SeatInspection` is `deny_unknown_fields`, and `retirement_state` is now present on every retired seat, so an older client would fail to decode one. `daemon/lifecycle.rs` already requires software version equality between CLI and daemon, and the herdr-graph source has no `SeatInspect` caller, so this skew is not reachable through either.
+- `SeatInspection` is not peer wire, and the wire-compat check passes.
+- `ReserveContinuityKey.seat` has no `serde(default)`, but a missing `Option` field reads as `None` under serde's derive, so a request omitting it decodes as the ordinary reservation.
+
+## Baseline trace within this cluster
+
+- B1: the key type, the three command fields, the capability and the reservation operations are present, and the dispatch to `continuity_keys::reserve` and `release` exists in `store/service_controls.rs`. Presentation by hook, watch and bridge, and the verdict itself, leave this cluster for the seam reviewer.
+- B2: `UnretireSeat` and `FinalizeRetirement` pass the session gate and dispatch to `reversible::unretire` and `reversible::finalize`. The result types and the `retired (reversible)` read surface are here. The service-side producer leaves this cluster; see the first item under the untested heading.
+- B3: nothing in this cluster changes what happens to memberships when an agent exits.
+- B4: nothing in this cluster changes a Herdr call or shape.
+
+## Verdict
+ready
+
+## Must fix before landing
+- none
